==Latin==

===Etymology===
The latter element is the common suffix {{af|la|-men}}; the former is from {{der|la|ine-pro|*gʷétu|t=pitch}} via an {{der|la|itc}} language in which *''gʷ'' became ''b'', e.g. {{bor|la|osc|-}} or {{bor|la|xum|-}}.<ref>{{R:cel:Matasovic 2009|65}}</ref> (The traditional derivation from Celtic is implausible as the related Celtic words—{{cog|sga|beithe}}, {{cog|cy|bedw}}, and the Gaulish source of {{cog|es|biezo}}—mean only ‘birch’, not ‘pitch’.)

Cognate with {{cog|gd|bìth|t=resin, gum}}, {{cog|en|cud}}, {{cog|sa|जतु|t=lac, gum}}. Influenced by {{m|la|ferrūmen||cement, glue}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|bitūmen}}

